    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-47357",
      "sequence_position": 16,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14194326/assets/64e35c559b233a28c5913ffa37ad949653ad2b46a0e10db639626b8575b7a263.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil, where $t>0$ is the time in hours since the leak started.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=2.$ Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of leaking is $2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$",
